# Civil Resistance and Violence against the Movement

**Authors:** Erica Chenoweth

## Abstract
<p>This chapter looks at myths and realities of civil resistance against brutal opponents. It
                        examines the ways in which civil resistance campaigns have succeeded in spite of episodes of
                        extreme brutality and the options available to movements fighting against racist regimes. It
                        explains why many people do not believe that civil resistance defeats a truly brutal opponent
                        and that only violence can overturn a tyrant who's willing to turn to mass slaughter,
                        imprisonment, torture, and other immoral and inhumane means to stay in power. The chapter argues
                        that where nonviolent civil resistance campaigns succeeded, it was only because the opponent was
                        not willing or able to use massive violence against them. That's a common argument about the
                        1989 Chinese student protests in Tiananmen Square, in which pro-democracy activists were
                        brutally crushed by the Chinese Army.</p>

**Source:** https://doi.org/10.1093/wentk/9780190244392.003.0005

